# EVERY ARITHMETIC CANDIDATE, AND WHETHER IT LANDS IN EXACTLY ONE VERDICT CLASS. One row per site.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ A COUNT-ONLY PARTITION CHECK IS INSUFFICIENT IN PRINCIPLE, AND THIS ONE IS BY KEY.
#    Σ|classes| = |candidates| passes when a tuple in two classes is cancelled by a tuple in none.
#    The classes must be pairwise disjoint AND jointly exhaustive, which is a statement about each
#    candidate, not about the totals. So: group by (site, filing, layer) and require exactly one.
#
# ⛔ NOT EXHAUSTIVE IS THE SILENT ONE. A candidate falling through every CASE branch takes a NULL
#   verdict, and the site then reports a smaller population and a cleaner verdict than it earned
#   -- the same failure `violates IS NULL` is guarded against in checks/.
#
# ⛔⛔⛔ SO THE NULL FILTER MUST SIT INSIDE THE COUNT AND NEVER BESIDE IT. As a `WHERE` it removes
#    the whole GROUP, and a candidate in no class stops being a candidate; as a `FILTER` it
#    leaves the group with ZERO classes and `classes <> 1` catches it. The two spellings are one
#    keyword apart and they make opposite claims about exhaustiveness.
#
#    ⭐ Demonstrated by dropping one `ELSE` from arithmetic/remainder.sqlc and recomposing:
#    `x_composed = F.Phi.x - e` falls from 16 candidates to 3, and with the filter beside the
#    count the law still returns `t` reading "3 candidates, 3 in exactly one class". With it
#    inside, eight of the nine sites go `f`. Restore the `ELSE` afterwards.
-- arithmetic/all.sqlc, one candidate to exactly one verdict.
SELECT a.law, p.subject, p.holds, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(algebra/roster.sqlc)
) a
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT z.site AS subject,
           count(*) FILTER (WHERE z.classes <> 1) = 0 AS holds,
           format('%s candidates, %s in exactly one class', count(*),
                  count(*) FILTER (WHERE z.classes = 1)) AS detail
    FROM ( SELECT site, filing, layer,
                  count(*) FILTER (WHERE w.verdict IS NOT NULL) AS classes
           FROM ( :compose(arithmetic/all.sqlc) ) w
           GROUP BY site, filing, layer ) z
    GROUP BY z.site
) p ON true
WHERE a.slug = 'arithmetic_class'
